[
  {
    "id": 1,
    "title": {
      "English": "Greetings",
      "Oshikwanyama": "Wa lele po?"
    },
    "description": "Greeting in Oshikwanyama is a sign of respect and should come before any  conversation. It’s customary to take your time and shake hands. When greeting a group, elders are always greeted first.\n\nTerms like Meme (mother), Tate (father), Meekulu (grandmother), and Tatekulu (grandfather) are respectful and commonly used when addressing others and are just as important as the greeting itself.\n\n'Wa lele po?' literally asks if someone 'laid down last night' (slept well), 'Wa uhala po?' asks about their afternoon, and 'Wa tokelwa po?' refers to how their evening went, literally 'Did the sky turn white for you'. The answer is always yes.",
